{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "156",
    "case_summary": "A 15-year-old boy with biopsy-proven Crohn disease. He was initially on infliximab therapy (5 mg/kg subcutaneous injection every 8 weeks) and later switched to adalimumab (40-mg/0.8-mL subcutaneous injection every 2 weeks) due to disease relapse.\n\n- Bilateral scaly, thickened, and erythematous upper and lower eyelids- Both lower eyelids were ectropic- Visual acuity was 20/20 OU- Crusty plaques on the eyelid margins - Diffuse conjunctival hyperemia- Rest of the ophthalmic examination was unremarkable\n\n{'Images': {'Title': 'Eyelid Manifestations Before and After Treatment', 'Description': 'A, Blepharitis and ectropion during TNF-α inhibitor treatment. B, Resolution after treatment discontinuation.'}}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Chronic anterior blepharitis\nB. TNF-α inhibitor-induced psoriasiform dermatitis\nC. TNF-α inhibitor–induced psoriasiform blepharitis\nD. Drug-induced blepharitis and ectropion associated with TNF-α inhibitors (infliximab and adalimumab)\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Bilateral Eyelid Inflammation with Ectropion and Conjunctival Hyperemia",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "Bilateral scaly, thickened, and erythematous upper and lower eyelids",
        "Ectropion of both lower eyelids",
        "Crusty plaques on the eyelid margins",
        "Diffuse conjunctival hyperemia"
      ],
      "excluded_candidates": [
        "Infectious conjunctivitis: less likely due to the presence of ectropion and crusty plaques",
        "Allergic conjunctivitis: possible, but the bilateral and symmetric nature of the symptoms makes it less likely"
      ],
      "alarm_features": [
        "Visual disturbance due to ectropion and conjunctival hyperemia"
      ],
      "salient_findings": [
        "Blepharitis",
        "Ectropion",
